Live-verified 2026-08-24 against a real AKI.IO /api/call response (capture 3 of the karr #101 run).

Captured body:

  {"text":"OK","success":true,"job_id":"a871d5bd-...-97ad","model_name":"Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct",
   "prompt_length":37,"num_generated_tokens":2,"num_cached_tokens":16,
   "current_context_length":39,"max_seq_len":65536,
   "total_duration":0.14,"compute_duration":0.123,"ep_version":3}

Replayed through Engine::AKI::chat_response (lib/Langertha/Engine/AKI.pm:262-273):

  content       OK
  model         Llama-3.1-8B-Instruct
  timing        total_duration=0.14
  id            -- UNSET --      although job_id is right there
  usage         -- UNSET --      although the token counts are right there
  finish_reason -- UNSET --

Three findings, in ascending order of blast radius:

1. id: job_id is the natural Response.id. Every other engine sets one; AKI does not.

2. usage: prompt_length 37 / num_generated_tokens 2 map onto Langertha::Usage
   input_tokens / output_tokens directly, and num_cached_tokens 16 is the same quantity
   as cached_tokens. Today ->usage is undef, so AKI native is invisible to every
   token-accounting consumer (Langfuse generations, Raider metrics, cost tracking).
   Note the counts disagree with the OpenAI shim for the same prompt (37 vs 64) -- worth
   a POD line, not a blocker.

3. total_duration UNIT COLLISION -- the interesting one. ADR 0011 declares engine-native
   stage keys unreserved by the framework, and two engines have now independently claimed
   the same key with different units:

     Engine::Ollama.pm  total_duration = NANOSECONDS (7319258765 in the shipped fixture),
                        and it derives total_seconds alongside, per ADR 0011
     Engine::AKI.pm:271 total_duration = SECONDS (0.14), and derives nothing

   So $response->timing->{total_duration} means ns on one engine and s on another. Any
   consumer reading the raw hashref -- which ADR 0011 explicitly sanctions for stage keys --
   is wrong by a factor of 1e9 depending on which engine answered. AKI additionally drops
   compute_duration 0.123 entirely.

   AKI should follow the Ollama precedent: emit total_seconds (already in seconds, no
   conversion) plus compute_seconds, and decide whether to keep the raw total_duration key
   at all -- it has no back-compat claim as strong as Ollama has, and keeping it is what
   perpetuates the collision. Role::Chat first-write-wins (Chat.pm:270) means a provider
   total_seconds correctly trumps the client-measured one.

   ADR 0011 consequence worth recording: "no engine-native key is reserved" implicitly
   assumed engine-native keys are read per-engine. This is the first proof that they are
   not namespaced and can collide semantically. Candidate for an ADR 0011 amendment
   rather than a silent fix -- raise with the adr-auditor lane.

